@node Journal Daily Note
@section The Note

A note this creates looks like this (shortened):

@example
:PROPERTIES:
:ID:       3c1f6d1e-8a0b-4c56-9a0e-0d6a2f4b7c11
:END:
#+title: 2026-03-10
#+description: Daily note for 2026-03-10, started from the activity journal.
#+authors: Zach Podbielniak
#+categories: dailies
#+created: 2026-03-10T20:00:00-0400
#+updated: 2026-03-10T20:00:00-0400
#+version: 1.0.0

* Activity
:PROPERTIES:
:CUSTOM_ID: cmacs-journal-daily
:CMACS_JOURNAL_DAILY: t
:CMACS_JOURNAL_UPDATED: 2026-03-10T20:00:00-0400
:END:
First activity 09:00, last 14:30. Active 3:20, idle 1:00, not recorded 1:10. ...

** Time by project
| Project | Time | %  |
|---------+------+----|
| beta    | 1:30 | 45 |
| alpha   | 1:28 | 44 |
| (gsurf) | 0:20 | 10 |
| (chat)  | 0:02 | 1  |

** Timeline
- 09:00--10:30 (1:28) alpha
- 10:30--10:50 (0:20) gsurf
- 13:00--14:30 (1:30) beta

** Files touched
- [[file:~/src/beta/beta.c][beta.c]] (1:30)

** Pages visited
- [[https://docs.gtk.org/glib/index.html][GLib Reference]] (0:20)

** Agent runs finished
- 14:10 reviewer: Review beta (done)
@end example

The sections, in order; one with nothing to say is left out:

@table @asis
@item (the first paragraph)
First and last activity, active time, recorded idle time, time not
recorded, and how many checkpoints.
@item Summary
A model's prose, when @code{cmacs-journal-daily-summary} is on, with a
line saying which model wrote it.
@item Time by day
Weekly notes only.
@item Time by context
Time per named context (@pxref{CMacs Context}), as the report by
@code{context}.  Only when a context applies to the range; otherwise
left out.
@item Time by project
As @code{cmacs-journal-report} by project.  Time outside any project is
shown under the application, in parentheses.
@item Time by application
As the report by app.
@item Time by site
Time in pages, by host.
@item Timeline
The day's blocks (below).  Daily notes only.
@item Longest focus stretches
The longest blocks, @code{cmacs-journal-daily-stretches} of them.
@item Files touched
Files that had the selected buffer, longest first, as @samp{file:}
links---or @samp{id:} links for notes.
@item Pages visited
URLs with their titles, longest first, as Org links.
@item Agent runs finished
The journal's @code{agent} events: when, which agent, the task's title,
how it ended.
@end table

Prose is never hard-wrapped: a paragraph is one line.  Tables are
aligned when they are written, so realigning one in Org changes
nothing.

@subheading Blocks

@cindex timeline blocks
The journal records spans: every change of focus ends one and starts
another.  A day is hundreds of them.  The timeline merges them into
@dfn{blocks}, each labelled by project, or by application where there
is no project:

@itemize @bullet
@item
spans with the same label no more than
@code{cmacs-journal-daily-block-gap} seconds apart (default 300) are one
block;
@item
so are two such spans with something else between them, when that
something else is over within the same gap---a minute in a chat window
in the middle of an afternoon's work is part of the afternoon.  The
time in brackets is the time actually spent on the label, without the
interruption;
@item
a block with less than @code{cmacs-journal-daily-block-min} seconds
(default 60) of active time is not listed.  Its time still counts in
every table.
@end itemize

Recorded idle time is not a span of anything, so it ends a block on its
own.

When named contexts were in use, a block also ends at a switch of
context, and its line ends with the context in brackets:
@samp{- 09:30--10:30 (0:58) alpha [writing]}.  A context's name is the
title of a journal event and goes through today's rules like every
other string: a rule can rename it, and a switch a rule excludes never
happened.

@subheading Links

@itemize @bullet
@item
A file is a @samp{[[file:...]]} link, by the name the journal recorded
(@file{~/...}).  No file is looked at to make it.
@item
A touched file that is itself a note---a local @file{.org} file inside
@code{cmacs-para-roots}---is linked by @samp{id:} instead, read from the
first 2000 characters of that file.
@code{cmacs-journal-daily-link-notes} @code{nil} turns this off, and
then nothing on disk is read at all.
@item
A URL is an Org link described by the page title.
@item
Projects are plain text.
@item
A name a privacy rule hashed or rewrote is no longer a path or a URL,
and stays plain text.
@end itemize

@node Journal Daily Existing
@section A File You Already Have

If the day's file exists---you write dailies by hand, or something else
does---nothing in it is overwritten or reordered.

@itemize @bullet
@item
The first run appends the subtree at the end of the file.  No header is
added, and the file's own @samp{#+updated:} is not changed.
@item
Every later run finds the subtree by its @code{CMACS_JOURNAL_DAILY}
property and replaces it where it stands.  Text before it and after it,
down to the blank lines, is byte for byte what you had.  You can move
the subtree anywhere in the file; it is found by the property, not by
position or title, so you can rename the heading too.
@item
When the facts in the file are already the facts of the day, nothing is
written at all: the file's modification time does not change.
@end itemize

What happens when the file is open in a buffer:

@table @asis
@item The buffer has unsaved edits
The subtree goes into the buffer, which stays modified.  The disk is
not touched.  A message says saving is left to you.
@item The buffer matches the disk
The same edit is made in the buffer and the file is written from it, so
both still agree.  Save hooks are not run.
@item The buffer was already out of date
The file is edited; the buffer is left as it was, and Emacs asks what
it always asks about a file changed on disk.
@end table

Save hooks are not run on purpose: a @code{before-save-hook} that
reformats or restamps a file would be touching ``everything else''.

A new note gets a bullet in the directory's @file{00_index.org}, once.
The bullet goes where the index's own layout says: under a heading for
the note's month (@samp{** March 2026}) among that month's bullets,
newest first unless they plainly run the other way; else under a new
heading for that month, placed among the other months; else after the
last @samp{id:} bullet.  A directory with no index gets one.  An
existing daily you wrote by hand is assumed to be in the index already
and is not added.

@node Journal Daily Summary
@section The Summary

@lisp
(setq cmacs-journal-daily-summary t)        ; default nil: facts only
(setq cmacs-journal-daily-provider 'ollama) ; the default
(setq cmacs-journal-daily-model "qwen3.5:9b")
@end lisp

The facts are written first, at once.  The model is asked
asynchronously (@code{cmacs-ai-chat-stream}); when it answers, a
@samp{** Summary} section is added to the same subtree, followed by a
line naming the provider and model.  If the model fails, is refused,
returns nothing or takes longer than
@code{cmacs-journal-daily-summary-timeout} seconds, the facts stay as
they are and a message says why there is no summary.

What the model is given is the facts---the lines of the sections above
as plain text---and the system prompt
@code{cmacs-journal-daily-summary-prompt}.  Nothing else: not the rest
of your note, not raw journal events, not window titles beyond what the
fact lists already show.

What it sends back is treated as untrusted text.  Reasoning printed
inside @samp{<think>} is removed, each paragraph becomes one line, a
line that would start a heading, a keyword or a drawer loses the
characters that would make it one, and the result goes through the
privacy rules once more.

@subheading Local, and what that means

@findex cmacs-journal-daily-provider-local-p
@code{cmacs-journal-daily-provider-local-p} decides.  Local are:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{ollama}, when the server it talks to is on a local host: what the
@env{OLLAMA_HOST} environment variable says, and unset means localhost;
@item
a name in @code{cmacs-ai-openai-compatible-endpoints} whose
@code{:base-url} is on a local host (a llama.cpp or vLLM of your own).
@end itemize

Everything else is not local: the hosted APIs, and the CLI agents
(@code{claude-code}, @code{codex-cli}, @dots{}), which call hosted
models.  Local hosts are @code{cmacs-journal-daily-local-hosts}
(@samp{localhost}, @samp{127.0.0.1}, @samp{::1}, any @samp{127.x}).
Add a machine of your own there only if a day's facts going to it is
the same to you as staying here.  When in doubt the answer is ``not
local''.

One limit: a base URL for ollama set in ai-glib's own configuration
file, or with @code{cmacs-ai-config-set-base-url}, cannot be read back
from Lisp today.  If you point ollama at another machine that way and
not with @env{OLLAMA_HOST}, this check does not see it.

@subheading A provider that is not local

@vindex cmacs-journal-daily-allow-remote
@code{cmacs-journal-daily-allow-remote} is its own option, apart from
the provider, so that changing the provider can never by itself send a
day anywhere.

@table @code
@item nil
The default.  The facts are written, the summary is left out, and the
message says why.  Nobody is asked.
@item t
A yes-or-no question naming the provider and saying it is not a local
model, each time.  An automatic note cannot ask: no summary.
@item always
Sent without asking, automatic notes included.
@end table

@node Journal Daily Privacy
@section Privacy

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Fail closed.}  While the journal's filter rules cannot be loaded
(@code{cmacs-journal-filter-ready-p} @code{nil}) nothing is read and
nothing is written.  The mode tries that day again at its next look.
@item
@strong{Rules are applied again.}  Every event read goes through
@code{cmacs-redact-event} as model-facing text does, before any total.
An event today's rules exclude is as if it had never been recorded: its
time is in no table.  A rule that rewrites a title rewrites it in the
note.  Then every line of the model prompt goes through
@code{cmacs-redact-text}, which also applies free-text rules
(@pxref{CMacs Redact}).
@item
@strong{This follows @code{cmacs-redact-mode}.}  With that mode off, or
its @code{prompt} surface removed, the second pass does nothing, as
everywhere else.  The spans on disk were still filtered when they were
recorded.
@item
@strong{A hash rule can hash twice.}  A field a rule hashed when it was
recorded is hashed again if the rule still matches on another field.
It is still a hash, and still groups the same way within one note.
@item
@strong{The note is a file in your notes.}  Whatever reads your
notes---an index, an agent with a notes tool, a sync---reads it.  That
is why the facts are filtered under today's rules too, not only the
prompt.
@item
@strong{No agent door.}  The commands signal an error for an agent or a
remote caller (@code{cmacs-journal-agent-context-p}), and the mode's
timer does nothing while one is being served.  An agent reading the
journal itself is governed by @code{cmacs-journal-expose-to-agents}, as
before.
@end itemize

@node Journal Daily When
@section When

@table @code
@item cmacs-journal-daily
@findex cmacs-journal-daily
Today's note.  With @kbd{C-u}, a date read the Org way
(@code{org-read-date}).
@item cmacs-journal-weekly
@findex cmacs-journal-weekly
The ISO week's note, @file{@var{YYYY}-W@var{ww}.org} in the same
directory.  With @kbd{C-u}, the week of a date.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-preview
@findex cmacs-journal-daily-preview
The subtree in a buffer.  Nothing is written and no model is asked.
@kbd{C-u} a date, @kbd{C-u C-u} the week.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-mode
@findex cmacs-journal-daily-mode
Global, off by default: writes notes from a timer.
@end table

@code{cmacs-journal-daily-mode} looks at the clock every
@code{cmacs-journal-daily-check-interval} seconds (300) and writes:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@emph{yesterday's note}, once the journal has recorded some activity
today (@code{cmacs-journal-daily-write-yesterday}, default on).  Waiting
for the first activity means yesterday's last span has closed, so the
note is the whole day;
@item
@emph{today's note} at @code{cmacs-journal-daily-time}
(@code{"HH:MM"}, default @code{nil}: not written).
@end itemize

Each is dealt with once per Emacs session.  Restarting Emacs looks
again, finds the same facts in the file and writes nothing---and does
not ask a model again for a summary that is already there.  A day with
no events writes nothing, silently.  All of it runs from a timer, never
from a compositor hook.

A day is local midnight to local midnight, cut at now for today.  A
span that crosses midnight is cut at midnight and belongs to both days,
a part to each.

Other doors, all thin:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@kbd{C-c C-d} in the @file{*cmacs-journal*} report buffer runs
@code{cmacs-journal-daily}.
@item
The voice commands ``write up my day'', ``write up my week'' and
``write up yesterday'' (@code{cmacs-journal-daily-voice}, registered
when voice commands load).
@end itemize

@node Journal Daily Options
@section Options

@table @code
@item cmacs-journal-daily-directory
Where notes go; relative to the first of @code{cmacs-para-roots}.
Default @code{"02_areas/dailies"}.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-heading
Heading of the owned subtree in a daily note.  Default
@code{"Activity"}.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-weekly-heading
The same, weekly.  Default @code{"Activity this week"}.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-author
@samp{#+authors:} of a new note; @code{nil} (the default) works it out.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-categories
@samp{#+categories:} of a new note.  Default @code{"dailies"}.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-link-notes
Link touched notes by @samp{id:}.  Default @code{t}.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-block-gap
Seconds of gap a block may span.  Default 300.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-block-min
Blocks shorter than this are not listed.  Default 60.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-max-rows
Rows per time table; the rest become @samp{(other)}.  Default 12.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-max-files
Files listed.  Default 25.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-max-urls
Pages listed.  Default 25.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-stretches
Longest stretches named.  Default 3.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-summary
Ask a model for a summary.  Default @code{nil}.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-provider
The cmacs-ai provider for it.  Default @code{ollama}.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-model
The model; @code{nil} for the provider's default.  Default
@code{"qwen3.5:9b"}.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-allow-remote
@code{nil} (the default), @code{t} (ask) or @code{always}.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-local-hosts
Hosts that count as this machine.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-summary-timeout
Seconds to wait for the model.  Default 600.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-summary-prompt
The system prompt.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-write-yesterday
The mode writes yesterday's note.  Default @code{t}.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-time
@code{"HH:MM"} at which the mode writes today's note.  Default
@code{nil}.
@item cmacs-journal-daily-check-interval
Seconds between the mode's looks at the clock.  Default 300.
@end table

Which kinds of event count as activity is the report's
@code{cmacs-journal-report-kinds}.

@node Journal Daily Internals
@section Internals

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{cmacs-journal-daily-facts} (@var{from} @var{to} &optional
@var{span}) returns everything the note says as a plist;
@code{cmacs-journal-daily-render} turns it into the subtree and
@code{cmacs-journal-daily-prompt} into the model's text.  Both render
from the same section list, which is how the prompt cannot hold what
the note does not.
@item
@code{cmacs-journal-daily-blocks} is the block merger, a single pass
with a stack.
@item
Every change to a file goes through one function that takes ``the
text'' and returns ``replace these characters by this string'', applied
to the visiting buffer or to the file as described above.  The note and
the index use the same one.
@item
The owned subtree is located with Org's own parser
(@code{org-find-property}, @code{org-end-of-subtree}) in a temporary
buffer with no hooks, on the text of the file or of the visiting
buffer---never by running Org commands in your buffer.
@item
A new @samp{:ID:} comes from @code{org-id-uuid}, not @code{org-id-new},
which runs @command{uuidgen} as a synchronous subprocess.
@item
A summary in flight is a job held by an integer id; the stream's
callback holds the id, so a job that timed out is gone and its late
callback finds nothing to do.
@end itemize

Tests: @file{test/cmacs/cmacs-journal-daily-tests.el}.  Every test binds
the journal directory and @code{cmacs-para-roots} to a temporary
directory; none writes into real notes, and the model is a stub.
